# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine last touched a
# file.
#
# Twenty-one tracked files were stored with CRLF and the rest with LF, because
# they were written on Windows and nothing told git to normalise them. On its
# own that is invisible. It stops being invisible the moment somebody on Linux
# clones the repository: their editor writes LF, git reports the whole file as
# changed, and a one-line fix arrives as a pull request that rewrites eight
# hundred lines. That is the kind of noise that makes a project look
# unmaintainable to the first person who tries to help.
#
# So: everything is stored as LF, and the files that genuinely need CRLF on
# Windows get it on checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the reason these are excepted. A .bat file with LF endings can
# mis-parse multi-line constructs on older Windows shells, and these scripts are
# the entry point for anyone building on Windows - the one place where a subtle
# failure costs the most. PowerShell is less fussy but shares the convention.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touched. Git's own detection usually gets these right; naming them means
# it cannot get them wrong.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.7z    binary
*.dll   binary
*.exe   binary
*.so    binary
*.lib   binary
*.arrow binary
*.parquet binary
*.lnk   binary
